[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":115},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1795740053000,"id","z45bgqa5kzmc6rcbca4x0g417","title","DOMDIFFER 以 DOM 镜像绕过安全控制，突破率连续居首","originalTitle","Most Wanted: The Phishing Kits and Techniques Getting Past Security Controls","summary","DOMDIFFER 是针对企业 Google 和 Microsoft 账号的 AiTM 钓鱼工具包，凭 DOM 镜像在 2026 年 8 月至 10 月连续以突破率居首，每月突破次数为次高工具包的 2-4 倍。","reason","source",{"_73":114},"links",{"_61":113},"publishedAt","2026-11-26T10:57:55.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-26T12:35:24.576Z","timelineAt","category","disclosure","tags",[74,109,110,111,112,78],"score",41,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_80":81,"_13":82},"x","readingMode","full","author","info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)","body",{"_105":-5,"_61":106,"_107":-5,"_108":36},"outline",[83,84,85,86,87,88],"relatedStories",[79],"topics",[68,69,70],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","fHaOEkaDNsiM","reportCount",1,"additionalSourceCount",0,{"_71":77,"_73":78},{"_71":75,"_73":76},{"_71":72,"_73":74},"slug","research","name","技术研究","email","邮件与钓鱼","browser","浏览器",{"_80":81,"_13":82},"publicId","76d8ce59-1f2c-4cd4-ba8f-917d8fa5f545","DOMDIFFER钓鱼工具突破率居首",{"_11":103,"_90":104,"_92":93},{"_11":101,"_90":102,"_92":93},{"_11":99,"_90":100,"_92":98},{"_11":96,"_90":97,"_92":98},{"_11":94,"_90":95,"_92":93},{"_11":89,"_90":91,"_92":93},"sec-6","text","What the webinar reveals","level",2,"sec-5","Why controls fall short","sec-4","Under the hood",3,"sec-3","TL;DR: DOM mirroring","sec-2","DOMDIFFER: Google's real sign-in page, mirrored from the attacker's browser","sec-1","How an attack breaks through","zh","<p><a href=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgOYXUiExbrrmBGGBgTjOFcDJSrLYJuITQwZY-l1mOzzkes21-mR0My9ikeCM7cI6VDXuBIE9BZPnKazemiuZf7A4w_xeeMy_IUUlNVTKX3PmW-Xt7FA9UI8JoICZDPW4ZWSspztPv_Mo4Zf7G_fXEw_phMe4uc89O38ugIlQJJKmc6VD7QN3NzU0n0SQU/s1600/push-most.jpg\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/7123d643b3bd11b4.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p><p>Every phishing attack has to get past email security, web filtering, and safe browsing before it reaches an employee. Some kits manage that far more often than others, and those are the kits that security teams need to know about.</p><p>At Push Security, we measure this as <strong>breakthrough rate</strong>: The share of attacks that get past email security, web gateways, and safe browsing to reach the user.</p><h2 id=\"sec-1\"><strong>How an attack breaks through</strong></h2><p>Every browser-based attack starts with a lure, and each control in its path sees only part of the attack:</p><ul>\n  <li><strong>Email security:</strong> Half of the attacks we see never touch email. The lure arrives through a search ad, a chat message, social media, a calendar invite, or a QR code. The rest come from trusted senders and services, so they pass reputation checks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Web gateways:</strong> The pages sit on trusted hosting and are assembled in the browser. The domains rotate every few days, so reputation data lags behind them.</li>\n  <li><strong>Safe browsing and scanners:</strong> Bot checks and cloaking show scanners a harmless page. A new domain isn't on any blocklist yet.</li>\n</ul><table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEg9CiS9QFJ_Qq7H-SYOIrtdKPjy_tDrr2p2cs6aZJc-8twBMJFdVySj7R7l74JJnJxy-z3rMj7jedYtBQP2NFE4_WitTxd-ehsLF4N3nMr20OHnHHEckCq2JA7vR5J8rlUkfbAjtAzl9mLqYDwFWxlJCVoS4g2baM9wQrc4cDORipKaNxJlyYJSE6UPuA4/s1600/02-attack-path.png\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/6b1312d984a22afe.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></td></tr><tr><td>How a phishing attack gets past email, URL, DNS, web gateway, and scanner controls before it reaches the user in the browser.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>When the page loads in the user's browser and the user interacts with it, that's a breakthrough. Breakthrough rate counts those attacks, not attempts.</p><p>This changes how to read upstream data. A kit with a high breakthrough rate is one that upstream controls are missing. A low count in email or proxy logs doesn't prove that a kit is rare, because the kit may be getting past those controls without a trace.</p><p>Our upcoming webinar reveals the five kits with the highest breakthrough rates. Here's an early look at the number one kit.</p><blockquote><a href=\"https://pushsecurity.com/webinar/most-wanted-phishing-kits?utm_campaign=58429501-Push%27s%20Most%20Wanted%20Webinar&amp;utm_source=the-hacker-news&amp;utm_medium=article\">Save your spot for the webinar</a></blockquote><h2 id=\"sec-2\"><strong>DOMDIFFER: Google's real sign-in page, mirrored from the attacker's browser</strong></h2><p>From August to October 2026, one kit ranked first by breakthrough rate every month. DOMDIFFER, an AiTM phishing kit that targets corporate Google and Microsoft accounts, broke through 2-4x as often as the next kit each month. DOM mirroring, the mechanism it uses under the hood, also happens to be the newest and fastest-growing way for a kit to relay a sign-in.</p><p>We first saw DOMDIFFER in February 2026. DOMDIFFER is our name for the kit (it hasn't yet been linked to a known phishing-as-a-service brand). We believe it grew out of NakedPages, a reverse proxy kit, and the two kits share infrastructure in several places. DOMDIFFER went quiet in the spring, came back over the summer, and has held the top rating since August. Most of its detections in that time came from fake calendar event lures.</p><table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgcypfeuzdR_4LJYhi5nWdfcY0P1HGE3arG1oEWB6NPnRjNTOVrHSpku4JQ0lB9FhfeK70nTXbwQBqmWa3pntMc0KJxd_r63S_6I2_3TRlA3VL822m4RhJJ0W_T68LWtfS1dFihZzr3mZaFLcKSCtE6T-57MJpsj6D68p6OERCTJBuIbt35zJKfW0kIZgg/s1600/03-domdiffer-hostnames.jpg\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/ea700da9f1523821.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></td></tr><tr><td>New hostnames per month for three NakedPages variants and DOMDIFFER, from our threat intelligence.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>One real DOMDIFFER attack starts with a fake Google Voice notification about a new voicemail.</p><table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEi4glY6EMhlxnJWwuORo8zHju6mRnjvB9xNfdIbxvM3DTTiVIzhxsGmRRSP4kT6kKIPlgtysl0JTOTRjpikJBVsYMIPL4xGokST-FMGqbKZ5QWTilqqT3-iamQIzICG6jNjtJetugODW9eDJdAuthrHAW7fVQ9jC6UZ0W7ZM3QEsK-MIhrvYucbtfxbC48/s1600/04-domdiffer-voicemail-lure.png\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/5ec52a6f7a0bafbb.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></td></tr><tr><td>The lure for one DOMDIFFER attack: A fake Google Voice voicemail notification.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The link passes a bot check that turns scanners away. The victim then sees what looks exactly like Google's sign-in page. It isn't a screenshot or a static copy. It's Google's real page, mirrored from the attacker's browser.</p><table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhPXc7QeljZAD9Na2SgvtuCF26wNl0zguG6ULC-guN1wCc00cO7N-iuGLR9rkUhEtqGuTnSsISbYoR7YUgc0TDuE1L90RDaM7LvL8vQVGpvYGZqJpq1RiVShYhXliIK9EjUKsbgftbHy6QKjWcdm-G4HGxIQdE3F3mlrFyHwhzIXERt2aY3U0dL7OLuq9I/s1600/05-domdiffer-mirrored-signin.png\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/691c55e18cf3212b.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></td></tr><tr><td>Google's sign-in page, mirrored from the attacker's browser by DOMDIFFER.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On the surface, this looks like fairly standard phishing: A notification lure, a bot check gate, then a sign-in page. What makes DOMDIFFER particularly effective lies beneath the surface.</p><h3 id=\"sec-3\"><strong>TL;DR: DOM mirroring</strong></h3><p>AiTM kits relay a sign-in in one of four ways: Reverse proxy, replica page, pixel streaming, and DOM mirroring. In DOM mirroring, the attacker's own browser does the real sign-in. It sends the page's structure (the DOM) to the victim's browser, which rebuilds a working copy. Because a real browser does the sign-in, the kit sends Google genuine browser signals and handles new sign-in steps without needing extra code.</p><p>Replica pages are still the most common class, but DOM mirroring is the newest and the fastest growing in 2026. Laurseraph, the main criminal pixel-streaming kit, has switched to DOM mirroring, and kits such as BlueKit and PhantomDOM use it too.</p><table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEjdQpO_3zl3Ygmxs-PcHf3BGNVDKDjPLbL9blRxr2QrKRC5fad2Oe90AAy-r7JGLBTYJePjZre87ud2oCJno-N3eDvdGezYr4oWCEizQmPMGKcMf-t6NgB-cZ1FN_WOsTkXrk8GXTVf8wzJMdg8mNSBBr-TvBxiXbuuI51O6CzQTQGkXDyXvNUBWvZonXo/s1600/06-aitm-classes-prevalence.png\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/764da8bf4731bc2b.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></td></tr><tr><td>How common each AiTM class is today, with example kits for each.</td></tr></tbody></table><table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgoWbfs_2tQqNHbFDctG9NNwc_7MLdE6M8Cjw2QYnZ_g7n0barhVBFOYbgdmRy0-7wtjhM7DZtuqVZqtTLs6RW2lU0E-9Sg__hl16k6yscxQBM-eSqs6vvaKE5JLs1Uhz27Vi0sEIZf4SvTjSIwDVqzVyVnKVNzKdssC_gwj4FD5C-OL5TockA9Mkw9Nrw/s1600/07-four-aitm-classes.jpg\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/07a49d5a1f7f9dfb.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></td></tr><tr><td>What the victim's browser gets in each AiTM class, and what relays the sign-in. Orange lines mark an attacker's browser in the middle.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Our research post,<a href=\"https://pushsecurity.com/blog/aitm-phishing-dom-mirroring?utm_campaign=58429501-Push%27s%20Most%20Wanted%20Webinar&amp;utm_source=the-hacker-news&amp;utm_medium=article\"> From reverse proxies to DOM mirroring</a>, explains the four classes in detail.</p><h3 id=\"sec-4\"><strong>Under the hood</strong></h3><p>DOM mirroring is only part of why DOMDIFFER breaks through security controls. The rest comes from how the kit reaches its victims and stays out of sight of the controls along the way. In the webinar on December 2, Luke will take apart DOMDIFFER and the other four kits in the top five. He'll show how each one works under the hood, and the tricks that get them past controls more often than other kits.</p><h2 id=\"sec-5\"><strong>Why controls fall short</strong></h2><p>Passkeys are worth rolling out, and they stop AiTM phishing where they're enforced. But AiTM is now one payload among several, and attackers are getting creative with how they combine them.</p><p>Kits use AiTM interchangeably with payloads that passkeys don't touch. In authorization attacks such as device code phishing, the victim signs in on Microsoft's real page and grants the attacker access. Other kits skip the sign-in page altogether and trick the victim into installing a remote access tool instead.</p><p>Kits pick the payload to match the visitor. A Windows visitor might get a remote access tool, a Microsoft account device code phishing, and a Google account AiTM. Some lures now carry two kits at once, so closing one path leaves another open.</p><p>Each of these gaps ends the same way: A page loads in the browser, and a user acts on it. In the webinar, Luke will show attackers use these tricks in real attacks.</p><h2 id=\"sec-6\"><strong>What the webinar reveals</strong></h2><p>DOMDIFFER is one of five kits that broke through most often from August to October. On December 2, Luke Jennings will reveal the other four in a live threat briefing. Join the webinar to see:</p><ul>\n  <li>The other four kits in the top five, and the techniques behind each one</li>\n  <li>How DOMDIFFER works under the hood, and the tricks that get it past controls</li>\n  <li>Real attacks from the top kits, from the lure to the stolen session</li>\n  <li>How kits now choose the payload to match the visitor's platform</li>\n  <li>Which techniques and kits should be on your radar heading into 2027</li>\n</ul><p>If you can't make it, register anyway and you'll get a copy of the recording.</p><p><span><a href=\"https://pushsecurity.com/webinar/most-wanted-phishing-kits?utm_campaign=58429501-Push%27s%20Most%20Wanted%20Webinar&amp;utm_source=the-hacker-news&amp;utm_medium=article\">Register for the webinar now</a>.</span></p><p>Found this article interesting? <span>This article is a contributed piece from one of our valued partners.</span> Follow us on <a href=\"https://news.google.com/publications/CAAqLQgKIidDQklTRndnTWFoTUtFWFJvWldoaFkydGxjbTVsZDNNdVkyOXRLQUFQAQ\">Google News</a>, <a href=\"https://twitter.com/thehackersnews\">Twitter</a> and <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/company/thehackernews/\">LinkedIn</a> to read more exclusive content we post.</p>","zhKind","complete","钓鱼","利用链","Microsoft","Google","https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/most-wanted-phishing-kits-and.html","The Hacker News",{"_116":117,"_118":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
